sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-45> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Mathematicians' intuitive descriptions of theorems (difficult, powerful, surprising, deep) reflect features of the network of statements connected by theorems, and fields regarded as more successful should have more compact networks while fields full of isolated facts, such as elementary number theory, should have sparser networks.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Judgments like deep or powerful reflect the structure of the theorem network";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Implications for Mathematics and Its Foundations” (from p. 772), p. 820. Scope: networks of theorems and proofs in fields of mathematics"
}
